GENERATE_SCENE_OUTLINE_PROMPT = '\nPlan a production-ready scene outline for Episode {{EpisodeIdx}}. Write all descriptive content in English.\n\n## World\n{{WorldView}}\n## Principal characters\n{{RoleInfo}}\n## Nearby episode outlines\n{{EpisodeOutline}}\n## Current episode outline\n{{Outline}}\n\n\n\n## Previous episode screenplay\n{{PrevEpisodeScript}}\n## Summaries of the last five episodes\n{{RecentEpisodeSummaries}}\n## Relevant narrative memory\n{{NarrativeMemory}}\n## Current character, relationship, asset, and environmental states\n{{StateMemory}}\n{% if PlotReference %}## Creative reference\n{{PlotReference}}\n{% endif %}\n\nPlanning principles:\n1. The current episode outline defines what this episode should accomplish. Add necessary process, resistance, and interaction, but do not complete events reserved for later episodes.\n{% if RecentEpisodeSummaries %}The previous two episode outlines are references only; use the last five episode summaries for what actually happened.{% endif %}\n2. Turn outline and memory constraints into what characters now want, what resists them, what they visibly do, and what follows. Their choices must emerge from circumstances and relationships, not mechanical obedience to the outline.\n3. Continue established events and retrieved states of characters, relationships, knowledge, object ownership, setting, and unfinished narrative business. If materials differ, use facts in completed screenplays and current state as the continuity baseline. Similarly, if the current outline conflicts with already established and dramatized events, adjust its specific details as needed to preserve narrative coherence while completing its core dramatic task.\n\n4. Aim for {{SceneNumsMin}}–{{SceneNumsMax}} scenes, adjusting to the drama. Start a new scene when time, place, or central action materially changes.\n5. In each scene, specify the goal, obstacle, visible action, outcome, and causal link to the next scene. Do not list only dialogue topics or abstract feelings.\n6. Plan only what can be filmed or heard. Externalize inner life through decisions, behavior, pauses, blocking, handling of objects, and subtext.\n7. Allocate the episode\'s target of {{WordNums}} words across scenes. End with a clear state change or forward drive. All plot twists should be supported by previously established character motivations.\n8. Build emotional anticipation and a fitting dramatic payoff in key scenes (for some genres, a cathartic payoff). Let action and consequence create a natural reason to watch the next scene or episode.\n\nOutput a valid JSON array only. The field names below are fixed internal schema keys; all field values must be in English:\n[\n  {\n    "scene_id": "{{EpisodeIdx}}-1",\n    "time_and_location": "Time and specific location",\n    "characters": ["Character name"],\n    "core_function": "This scene\'s function in the episode\'s causal chain",\n    "plot_summary": "Full dramatic action and outcome",\n    "character_goals": [{"character": "Character name", "motivation_and_goal": "Immediate goal and its cause"}],\n    "conflicts_and_tension": ["Obstacle, disagreement, or cost"],\n    "key_actions": ["Visible actions in chronological order"],\n    "ending_hook": "An unresolved outcome leading onward, or a state change if suspense would be forced"\n  }\n]\n'
GENERATE_WHOLE_EPISODE_PROMPT = "\nWrite the complete, filmable English screenplay for Episode {{EpisodeIndex}}.\n\n## World\n{{WorldView}}\n## Principal characters\n{{RoleInfo}}\n## Summaries of the last five episodes\n{{RecentEpisodeSummaries}}\n## Relevant narrative memory\n{{PrevAbs}}\n## Current character, relationship, asset, and environmental states\n{{StateMemory}}\n## Current episode outline\n{{EpisodeOutline}}\n## Complete scene outline for this episode\n{{AllSceneOutlines}}\n\n\n\n## Ending of the previous episode\n{{PrevPlot}}\n{% if PlotReference %}## Creative reference\n{{PlotReference}}\n{% endif %}\n\nCore requirements:\n1. The episode outline defines the dramatic task; the scene outline defines how it unfolds. Add necessary action and interaction, but do not change core outcomes, omit key causal steps, or enact later episodes early.\n2. Constraints from memories of past events are used only behind the scenes to maintain continuity. Preserve continuity of identity, allegiance, character and world states, relationships, knowledge, time and place, ownership and function of objects, and event causes and outcomes. Show a credible trigger and process for every change.\n3. If the current outline conflicts with already established and dramatized events, adjust its details to preserve story-wide coherence while accomplishing its central dramatic task.\n\n4. Reveal character through choices, actions, and subtext. Dialogue grows from relationships, immediate circumstances, and competing interests; avoid self-description, exposition dumps, and repetition.\n5. Write only what a camera can see or a microphone can hear. Externalize inner life through gestures, blocking, object handling, pauses, avoidance, and reactions to the environment. Actions must change the situation.\n6. Give each scene a complete dramatic progression: a goal, resistance, a choice, and an outcome. Link scenes causally. Do not stack conflicts mechanically or sacrifice character logic for stimulation or twists.\n7. Aim for the information density and narrative momentum of short-form drama: let dialogue and action introduce meaningful new information, change relationships, or advance conflict, while avoiding repeated expressions of the same emotion or position. At key revelations or moments of decision, moderately heighten character reactions and show the immediate consequences, so the audience can clearly feel the episode’s progression and emotional payoff.\n8. Write every planned scene. Keep the episode within the {{WordNums}}-word target. Make the ending's state change and forward drive concrete.\n9. Let escalating resistance, character choice, and its consequence build anticipation and a fitting dramatic payoff (for some genres, a cathartic payoff). Place a forward hook where it naturally belongs.\n\n## Output format\n{{ScriptFormat}}\n\nOutput only this episode's complete screenplay, without analysis, production notes, or Markdown.\n"
